// tap-echo: --buffer 65535
// Ensure that the reception of a TCP RST with SEG.SEQ = RCV.NXT
// in the LAST-ACK state destroys the TCP connection.
// Adapted from freebsd-net/tcp-testsuite commit 04b8c73fc5081fe3792f1bed9168c9b22aaedddb,
// state-event-engine/rcv-rst-last-ack/rcv-rst-last-ack-left-edge-secure-ipv4.pkt
// (BSD-2-Clause; notice below). Changes for Zhida's packet-only runner, tap-echo
// being the application (fuzz/packetdrill/README.md, "Upstream suites"):
// - IPv4 variant. Dropped: sysctls, which set FreeBSD defaults and
//   net.inet.tcp.insecure_rst=0 (RFC 5961 RST checks on); Zhida has no such switch;
//   fcntl; getsockopt(SO_RCVBUF); --tolerance_usecs=75000 (the runner keeps
//   packetdrill's 4 ms); sockstat and tcpdrop commands except where replaced below.
// - Upstream reaches the state by an active open (connect; FreeBSD's SYN, the
//   peer's SYN-ACK <mss 1460,sackOK>, FreeBSD's ACK). tap-echo only listens, so the
//   client opens instead: the client's SYN carries the options upstream's peer sent
//   (same negotiated result: MSS 1460, SACK, no window scale, no timestamps), as in
//   upstream's own passive-open scripts (rcv-rst-syn-rcvd via-listen), then Zhida's
//   SYN-ACK and the client's ACK 0.1 s later.
// - shutdown(SHUT_WR) after the client's FIN: tap-echo closes as soon as the peer has
//   closed and nothing is left to echo, so Zhida's FIN follows its ACK of the client's
//   FIN, as upstream's.
// - Receive window: tap-echo --buffer 65535, so Zhida's RCV.WND is 65535, its
//   advertised window (no window scale), as upstream's.
// - The connection is gone (upstream: getsockopt(SO_ERROR) = ECONNRESET or sockstat
//   showing no SYN_RCVD socket): 0.1 s later a SYN on the same 4-tuple gets a SYN-ACK
//   from the listener tap-echo reopens (ISN 0 relative to this handshake;
//   scripts/echo-and-close.pkt). A surviving connection would not answer it.
//
